B.akE=new A.ca("Bloch Sphere","The Bloch sphere shows the state of a qubit as a point on a sphere. Here, it's the blue dot.\nApplying a gate rotates that point around the matching axis.\nGo ahead and try one gate first.")
B.akH=new A.ca("Applying Gates","See how the blue dot rotated around the gate's axis? You can also swipe left and right to change your view of the Bloch sphere, so try the other gates too.")
B.ale=new A.ca("Next: Probability Amplitude","Now let's look at probability amplitude. Tap \"Probability Amplitude\" when you're ready.")
B.akY=new A.ca("Probability Amplitude","A qubit can sit somewhere between 0 and 1, and its state is described by complex numbers. A probability amplitude tells you how strongly 0 or 1 shows up when you measure the qubit.\nNote: In this game, we drop the global phase (the phase shared by every component), adjust the state so the first non-zero amplitude is a positive real number, and show the result as real amplitudes (-1 to 1).")
B.akW=new A.ca("Probability","Square a probability amplitude and you get the probability, which is the chance of seeing that outcome when you measure.\nTry different gates and compare each piece's probability amplitude with its probability.")
B.a06=s([B.akX,B.akE,B.akH,B.ale,B.akY,B.akW],t.dH)
B.al2=new A.ca("Quantum Computing with 2 Cells","On this page, you'll explore the state of two qubits through probability amplitudes and probabilities.")
B.akM=new A.ca("Representing Two Qubits",'With one qubit, you had two options: the |0\u27e9 state or the |1\u27e9 state. With two qubits, the first can be |0\u27e9 or |1\u27e9 and so can the second, so together they make four possible states.\nIn notation like "|00\u27e9", where two digits sit inside | \u27e9, the left digit is the first qubit and the right digit is the second.')
B.akI=new A.ca("Try It Out","Use this 2-cell board to apply gates, create superpositions, and entangle the cells. Watch how the state of the two qubits changes as you go.")
B.a5U=s([B.al2,B.akM,B.akI],t.dH)
B.akU=new A.ca("Grover's Algorithm with 3 Cells","On this page, you'll try out \"Grover's algorithm,\" one of the algorithms unique to quantum computers, using a 3-cell board and three gates.")
B.ali=new A.ca("What Is Grover's Algorithm?",'Grover\'s algorithm is a quantum algorithm that speeds up the "search problem": finding a specific value in a database with lots of entries. It really shines when you need to pick out one particular value from a huge pile of data.')
B.akL=new A.ca("Number of States","Here we'll apply the algorithm to 3 cells, which means 3 qubits. With 3 qubits, there are 2\xb3 = 8 possible quantum states.")
B.akR=new A.ca("The Target","Our goal is to find the |111\u27e9 state among the 8 possibilities.")
B.al3=new A.ca("A New Gate (CCZ)","To find |111\u27e9, we need two new gates. First up is CCZ (Control-Control-Z). It works like CNOT (Control-X): it applies Z to the third bit only when the first and second bits are both black |1\u27e9. That lets us mark the |111\u27e9 we're looking for.")
B.alc=new A.ca("A New Gate (Diff)",'The other is the Diff (Diffusion / inversion) gate. For this 3-qubit search for |111\u27e9, Diff can be built as the combined gate H\u2192X\u2192CCZ\u2192X\u2192H.\n[Diff = H\u2192X\u2192CCZ\u2192X\u2192H]\nIt flips each state\'s probability amplitude "around the average," which boosts the amplitude of the marked target state.')
B.alg=new A.ca("Quantum Circuit","Time to run the algorithm! Follow this quantum circuit and apply the gates to the board.")
B.al1=new A.ca("Superposition","Apply H to every cell to put the whole board into a superposition.")
B.al0=new A.ca("Marking the Answer","Following the circuit, CCZ marks the number we're looking for by flipping the sign of its probability amplitude.")
B.alj=new A.ca("Inverting","The Diff gate flips the marked number's probability amplitude around the average.")
B.akT=new A.ca("One Round Complete","You've run Grover's algorithm once, and the probability of |111\u27e9 is now up to 78%. One more round will push the probability of our target state to its peak. Let's keep going!")
B.akP=new A.ca("Two Rounds Complete","After two rounds of Grover's algorithm, the probability is up to 95%. Now measure to pull out the state you're looking for.")
B.akO=new A.ca("Measurement Complete","Out of 8 candidates, we found the target |111\u27e9 in just a few steps.\nThat's the power of quantum properties like superposition: they can help solve certain problems efficiently.")
B.akS=new A.ca("Grover's Algorithm","We searched for |111\u27e9 with 3 qubits this time, but with more qubits and a circuit that marks the target state, you can search through far more candidates efficiently.\nQuantum computers have plenty of other fascinating algorithms, so keep exploring and learning!")
B.a8c=s([B.akU,B.ali,B.akL,B.akR,B.al3,B.alc,B.alg,B.al1,B.al0,B.alj,B.akT,B.akP,B.akO,B.akS],t.dH)
B.a_y=s(["In general, the amplitudes of a quantum state are complex numbers, but with the initial states and gates used in this game (H, X, Y, Z, CNOT, SWAP, CCZ), the imaginary unit i only ever shows up as a global phase.","A global phase is a phase factor (e^{i\u03c6}) that multiplies every component of the state vector. It doesn't change measurement probabilities or any physical prediction, so you can't observe it.","To keep things easy to read, the game always adjusts the global phase to 1 before showing the state."],t.s)
B.nb=new A.a29(B.a06,B.a5U,B.a8c,"Thanks for sticking with it! We hope the board and the Bloch sphere gave you at least a little feel for the quantum world.","How This App Handles Phase",B.a_y,"To Learn More","If this sparked your curiosity, try an introductory book or lecture videos next. They cover, step by step and with the math, the parts this game couldn't. We hope it's a step toward a deeper understanding of quantum computing.","\u2022")
B.al9=new A.ca("1\u30de\u30b9\u3067\u5b66\u3076\u91cf\u5b50\u30b3\u30f3\u30d4\u30e5\u30fc\u30bf","\u3053\u306e\u30da\u30fc\u30b8\u3067\u306f\u30011\u91cf\u5b50\u30d3\u30c3\u30c8\u306b\u5bfe\u5fdc\u3059\u308b1\u30de\u30b9\u76e4\u9762\u306b\u5bfe\u3057\u3066\u3001\u30d6\u30ed\u30c3\u30db\u7403\u3068\u78ba\u7387\u632f\u5e45\u30b0\u30e9\u30d5\u3092\u7528\u3044\u3066\u91cf\u5b50\u72b6\u614b\u3092\u76f4\u611f\u7684\u306b\u5b66\u3073\u307e\u3059\u3002")
